# Use-case 10 gap 6 (claim C4, silent half): including a type()d
# schema that references a type()d named alias silently DROPS the data
# record from generation, exit 0.  The value survives in the graph:
# `--canon` still shows customers.c1, and adding
# `out: copy($.customers.c1)` emits {"ledgerId":5} — copy() clears
# marks, proving a leaked type mark hides the record.  The identical
# statements in ONE file (no include), or this include with
# `ledgerId: integer` inline (no alias), emit the record correctly —
# exactly the documented pattern (how-to.md, "Keep schema/helper
# fields out of the output").
# expected: {"customers":{"c1":{"ledgerId":5}},"schema":{}} exit 0
# actual:   {"customers":{},"schema":{}} exit 0 (record silently gone)
@"./alias-vocab.aon"
customers: c1: { ledgerId: 5 }
